::: {.doc-subject}

Check if the graph contains any cycles.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L32330-L32395){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.has_cycles()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Has cycles](../assets/graphs/has-cycles.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: a directed graph on five nodes containing the cycle B, E, C, B (shown in the accent color), so has_cycles() returns True. Right: the same graph with the edge between B and C pointing the other way (shown in the accent color); no path returns to its start any more, so has_cycles() returns False." width="100%"}

For directed graphs, uses depth-first search to look for back edges. For undirected graphs,
walking an edge and straight back does not count: a cycle needs three or more distinct
nodes, a self-loop, or two parallel edges between the same pair of nodes. Trees and paths
are therefore acyclic.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph contains at least one cycle, `False` if acyclic.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~simple_cycles`) 

:   Every cycle in the graph.

[](`~is_dag`) 

:   Check if a directed graph has no cycles.

[](`~girth`) 

:   The length of the shortest cycle.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A chain of arrows has no cycle until an arrow leads back to its start:

::: {#a12d2a24 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

chain = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("B", "C")], directed=True)
loop = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A")], directed=True
)

chain.has_cycles(), loop.has_cycles()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(False, True)
```
:::
:::


### Which molecules have rings

`molecules()` holds seven small molecules as graphs of their atoms and bonds, with the
hydrogen atoms left out. A molecule has a ring of atoms exactly when its graph has a
cycle:

::: {#bd4f79ec .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
molecules = ec.molecules()

{name: molecule.has_cycles() for name, molecule in molecules.items()}
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
{'benzene': True,
 'cyclohexane': True,
 'ethanol': False,
 'dimethyl_ether': False,
 'butane': False,
 'isobutane': False,
 'caffeine': True}
```
:::
:::


